%T A402155 0,1,8,0,-4,-3,0,1,-2,0,2,-1,0,3,9,0,-1,0,1,10,0,-2,-1,0,1,0,2,11,-5,
%U A402155 0,-1,0,1,0,12,-4,0,1,-1,0,2,-6,0,-2,0,1,3,-1,0,4,0,13,-1,0,1,-3,0,-2,0,1
%N A402155 Horizontal coordinates where successive walks from (0,1) first reach the x-axis, guided by arrows that initially point south and turn 90 degrees counterclockwise each time they are followed.
%C A402155 Every lattice point (x,y) with y >= 1 carries an arrow, and at the start all arrows point south. Walkers are released from (0,1) one after another, each one after the previous walker has stopped. At each step a walker moves one unit in the direction of the arrow at its point, and the arrow at the point it has just left then turns 90 degrees counterclockwise (south, east, north, west, south, ...). The walker stops when it first reaches a point (x,0), and a(n) is this x for the n-th walker. The arrows are not reset: each walker starts with the arrows as the earlier walkers left them. Every walker stops.
%C A402155 Nothing in the rule is random, yet the terms are distributed like the landing points of a random walker. Let q(m) be the probability that a random walker who starts at (0,1) and at each step moves, independently of its earlier steps, to one of the four neighboring lattice points, each with probability 1/4, first reaches the x-axis at (m,0). Let c(N,m) be the number of n <= N with a(n) = m. Then c(N,m)/N tends to q(m) as N tends to infinity; for example, the proportion of zero terms tends to q(0) = 1 - 2/Pi = 0.3633... The proportions of positive and of negative terms both tend to 1/Pi (A049541), and the proportion of terms that are even numbers tends to 2 - sqrt(2) (A101465). Every integer occurs infinitely often.
%C A402155 For each fixed integer m, the number of N independent random walkers that land at (m,0) has standard deviation sqrt(N*q(m)*(1-q(m))), of order sqrt(N). For this sequence the agreement is far closer: c(N,m) differs from N*q(m) by at most a constant times log(N+1), where the constant does not depend on m or N. For example, c(10^8,0) = 36338023, and 10^8*(1 - 2/Pi) = 36338022.76...
%C A402155 Although q(-m) = q(m), the sequence is not symmetric under a change of sign. After N walkers exactly N arrows point east or north, and a(1) + ... + a(N) is the number of arrows that point north or west (the four moves made from a point during a full turn of its arrow cancel, so only incomplete turns count). Hence every partial sum is nonnegative, although negative and positive terms have the same limiting proportion. The values a(1), ..., a(N) fill an interval of integers. For m >= 1, c(N,m) and c(N,-m) differ by at most a constant times log(m+1), where the constant does not depend on m or N.
%C A402155 Conjectures on the counts: (1) c(N,m) and c(N,-m) never differ by more than 1; (2) among a(1), ..., a(N) there are always at least as many positive as negative terms; (3) every m >= 1 first appears before -m does (10 first appears at n = 50, and -10 at n = 213); (4) for each fixed m, c(N,m) - N*q(m) remains bounded as N grows. For N <= 10^8, (1) and (2) hold and c(N,0) - N*q(0) lies between -0.91 and 1.22; for 1 <= m <= 6436, (3) holds.
%C A402155 Conjectures on the growth, as N -> oo: (5) the largest of a(1), ..., a(N) is asymptotic to sqrt((sqrt(2)+1)*N) and the smallest to -sqrt((sqrt(2)-1)*N), so that their product is asymptotic to -N (for N = 10^8 they are 15536 and -6436, with product -99989696); equivalently, since the values fill an interval, the first n with a(n) = m and the first n with a(n) = -m are asymptotic to (sqrt(2)-1)*m^2 and (sqrt(2)+1)*m^2, respectively, as m -> oo (for m = 1000 they are 414797 and 2410823); (6) the number of distinct lattice points (x,y) with y >= 1 visited by the first N walkers is asymptotic to (13 - 5*sqrt(2))/4*N, and the largest y-coordinate reached by these walkers is asymptotic to sqrt((2*sqrt(2)-2)*N) (for N = 10^8 these are 148216969 and 9102); (7) (a(1) + ... + a(N))/N tends to 1 (a(1) + ... + a(10^8) = 99968431). See A278928, A154747 and A388753 for sqrt(sqrt(2)+1), sqrt(sqrt(2)-1) and sqrt(2*sqrt(2)-2).
%C A402155 The pictures in the Links section show the arrows after 10^6 walkers: large triangular regions of equally directed arrows around an irregular central region. Walks guided by turning arrows are called rotor walks or rotor-router walks (Priezzhev, Dhar, Dhar and Krishnamurthy; Propp). The notes file in the Links section proves that every walker stops, the arrow-count identities, the interval property, the limiting proportions, the logarithmic bounds and the statements of the Formula section, and gives sources and further data. The proofs about c(N,m) use a method of Holroyd and Propp, and the probabilities q(m) and their integral formula are classical (Spitzer, Section 15; Lawler and Limic, Section 8.1).
%D A402155 Frank Spitzer, Principles of Random Walk, 2nd edition, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 34, Springer-Verlag, 1976, Section 15.
